p": [[" documentation", -0.6076175570487976], [" output", -1.289963960647583]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.37687036395072937, "top": [["\n", -0.37687036395072937], [" for", -1.8397284746170044]]}, {"t": "-", "lp": -0.0006325456779450178, "top": [["-", -0.0006325456779450178], ["-D", -11.01793384552002]]}, {"t": " Recent", "lp": -1.2435493469238281, "top": [[" Recent", -1.2435493469238281], [" Your", -1.3023815155029297]]}, {"t": " Intel", "lp": -1.5067576169967651, "top": [[" Intel", -1.5067576169967651], [" kernel", -1.5635985136032104]]}, {"t": " GPU", "lp": -0.6050493121147156, "top": [[" GPU", -0.6050493121147156], [" Arc", -1.5453016757965088]]}, {"t": " mailing", "lp": -0.6617556810379028, "top": [[" mailing", -0.6617556810379028], [" driver", -0.7996912598609924]]}, {"t": " list", "lp": -0.02716013230383396, "top": [[" list", -0.02716013230383396], [" lists", -4.17158842086792]]}, {"t": " discussions", "lp": -0.02376071736216545, "top": [[" discussions", -0.02376071736216545], [" threads", -4.705691337585449]]}, {"t": "\n\n", "lp": -0.13544459640979767, "top": [["\n\n", -0.13544459640979767], [",", -3.0256481170654297]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing / preemption-based**\n- CCS contexts are allocated dynamically from the available CCS units\n- The driver manages context switching between GCS (General Compute Slices) and CCS\n- More flexible but can have overhead from context management\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Static/dedicated allocation**\n- CCS units are statically assigned to contexts\n- Better for workloads that want guaranteed dedicated compute resources\n- Lower overhead since there's no dynamic context stealing\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **Disabled / no CCS usage**\n- Forces all compute to use GCS only\n- CCS units are completely bypassed\n\n## When to use each\n\n**Mode 1** \u2014 Default choice for most workloads. Good general-purpose balance.\n\n**Mode 2** \u2014 Use when you need deterministic, dedicated compute resources, or when profiling shows context-stealing overhead is a bottleneck.\n\n**Mode 4** \u2014 Use when:\n- You're debugging driver issues related to CCS handling\n- A specific workload or application has problems with CCS contexts\n- You want to isolate whether CCS is causing issues\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I want to be transparent that my knowledge here is somewhat limited and I may not have the precise, driver-specific semantics correct. The `ccs_mode` parameter is fairly niche, and the exact behavioral differences between modes 1, 2, and 4 can vary between driver versions.\n\nI'd recommend verifying against:\n- The actual kernel driver source (`drivers/gpu/driver/xe/`)\n- `modinfo xe` documentation\n- Recent Intel GPU mailing list discussions\n\n"}